WEBVTT

NOTE Visual text from a silent video.

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.400
Policy allows five calls for this illustrative task.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:14.100
After the fifth call, a sixth proposal must not execute.

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:22.000
Return partial status or escalate the case; do not invent success.

4
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:30.100
A 429 calls for waiting; repeating an invalid argument does not fix it.

5
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:37.500
A timeout can hide an operation that actually executed.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:46.000
Choose waiting, correction or state inspection according to the contract.

7
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:54.100
Request K7 creates an operation, but its response is lost.

8
00:00:54.100 --> 00:01:01.500
The retry keeps K7, and the API returns the existing operation.

9
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:10.000
That guarantee depends on the system’s idempotency contract, not the prompt.

10
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.400
Once retries are exhausted, the operation must leave “running”.

11
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:24.100
Mark terminal failure and route the case to an owned queue or review.

12
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:32.000
Do not leave a task indefinitely pending with no owner.

13
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:40.100
Two tools work while conversation continues on its own turn.

14
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:47.500
The first result does not close a batch that still has pending work.

15
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:56.000
Once the batch finishes, deliver one completion through the chosen route.

16
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:03.400
When the path is already known, a function or workflow can solve it.

17
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:10.100
Reserve model decisions for the part that depends on the situation.

18
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:18.000
Keep boundaries, verification and an operable fallback around that part.
